From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: David Heinemeier Hansson <david@hey.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 12:55:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] soundwire: qcom: re-enumerate v3.1.0 controllers on runtime
 resume

Glymur's two WSA SoundWire controllers, the only v3.1.0 instances,
runtime suspend by stopping the bus clock and resume by exiting clock
stop. On the ASUS Zenbook A16 (UX3607OA) that exit leaves one bus's
WSA884x amplifiers unattached, or attached with every register reading
0x21, in about two resumes out of three (10 of 15 idle/play cycles),
silencing that pair of speakers until the next stop. The amplifier
driver then reads its PA state as faulted and leaves the PA disabled.

Probe's full reset and enumeration has always brought all four
amplifiers up, so take that path on resume too: mark clock stop as
unsupported on v3.1.0, which makes runtime suspend skip the clock stop
and runtime resume soft-reset the controller, re-initialise it and wait
for enumeration. With this all four amplifiers came back attached, with
valid registers, in 15 of 15 cycles, and sound started after idle no
later than with runtime suspend disabled.
---
 drivers/soundwire/qcom.c | 8 +++++++-
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/soundwire/qcom.c b/drivers/soundwire/qcom.c
--- a/drivers/soundwire/qcom.c
+++ b/drivers/soundwire/qcom.c
@@ -664,7 +664,13 @@

 		sdw_extract_slave_id(bus, addr, &id);
 		found = false;
-		ctrl->clock_stop_not_supported = false;
+		/*
+		 * Peripherals on v3.1.0 controllers (Glymur) often come back
+		 * from a clock stop unattached, or attached but returning
+		 * garbage for every register read. Reset the controller and
+		 * re-enumerate on runtime resume instead, as at probe.
+		 */
+		ctrl->clock_stop_not_supported = ctrl->version >= SWRM_VERSION_3_1_0;
 		/* Now compare with entries */
 		list_for_each_entry_safe(slave, _s, &bus->slaves, node) {
 			if (sdw_compare_devid(slave, id) == 0) {
